1
00:00:02,090 --> 00:00:04,200
Previously on The Big Bang Theory...

2
00:00:04,230 --> 00:00:05,720
I am going to the Arctic Circle

3
00:00:05,780 --> 00:00:07,850
with Leonard, Walowitz, and Koothrappali.

4
00:00:07,920 --> 00:00:09,600
- For three months?  - Yes.

5
00:00:10,100 --> 00:00:13,560
What did you mean when you said you were going to miss me?

6
00:00:15,710 --> 00:00:17,700
It means I wish you weren't going.

7
00:00:32,100 --> 00:00:33,600
Oh, thank God we're home.

8
00:00:33,600 --> 00:00:37,640
I can't believe we spent three months in that frozen hell.

9
00:00:37,640 --> 00:00:39,150
It was like a snowy nightmare

10
00:00:39,150 --> 00:00:41,100
from which there was no awakening.

11
00:00:41,970 --> 00:00:45,000
I don't know what Arctic expedition you guys were on,

12
00:00:45,070 --> 00:00:47,110
but I thought it was a hoot and a half.

13
00:00:49,540 --> 00:00:51,050
Oh, hi, Mom.

14
00:00:51,630 --> 00:00:54,220
No, I told you I'd call you when I got home.

15
00:00:54,220 --> 00:00:56,400
I'm not home yet.

16
00:00:56,400 --> 00:00:58,580
All right, I'm home.

17
00:00:59,670 --> 00:01:02,400
The Arctic expedition was a remarkable success.

18
00:01:02,470 --> 00:01:04,860
I'm all but certain there's a Nobel Prize in my future.

19
00:01:04,920 --> 00:01:06,180
Actually, I shouldn't say that.

20
00:01:06,240 --> 00:01:07,950
I'm entirely certain.

21
00:01:08,160 --> 00:01:11,170
No, Mother, I could not feel

22
00:01:11,170 --> 00:01:13,580
your church group praying for my safty.

23
00:01:14,610 --> 00:01:16,400
The fact that I'm home safe does not prove that it worked.

24
00:01:16,400 --> 00:01:18,280
The logic is post hoc ergo propter hoc.

25
00:01:20,510 --> 00:01:24,300
No, I'm not sassing you in Eskimo talk.

26
00:01:24,380 --> 00:01:26,420
I'm gonna go let Penny know we're back.

27
00:01:26,420 --> 00:01:27,820
Mother, I have to go.

28
00:01:27,820 --> 00:01:30,040
Yeah, love you. Bye.

29
00:01:30,940 --> 00:01:33,640
Hello, old friend.

30
00:01:41,940 --> 00:01:43,490
Daddy's home.

31
00:01:45,610 --> 00:01:47,830
Leonard, you're back.

32
00:01:47,900 --> 00:01:50,020
Yeah. I just stopped by to say-- hmph!

33
00:01:55,100 --> 00:01:57,160
- Yeah. So, hi.  - Hi.

34
00:02:05,230 --> 00:02:08,700
Damn it, I should have gone over and told her we were back.

35
00:02:14,130 --> 00:02:16,170
Yeah, it was first-come, first-serve.

36
00:02:43,800 --> 00:02:45,010
I just want you both to know,

37
00:02:45,010 --> 00:02:46,450
When I publish my findings,

38
00:02:46,450 --> 00:02:48,490
I won't forget your contributions.

39
00:02:48,490 --> 00:02:49,850
- Great.  - Thanks.

40
00:02:50,530 --> 00:02:53,020
Of course, I can't mention you in my Nobel acceptance speech,

41
00:02:53,020 --> 00:02:54,760
but when I get around to writing my memoirs,

42
00:02:54,760 --> 00:02:56,470
you can expect a very effusive footnote

43
00:02:56,470 --> 00:02:58,020
and perhaps a signed copy.

44
00:02:59,290 --> 00:03:01,280
We have to tell him.

45
00:03:01,280 --> 00:03:02,680
Tell me what?

46
00:03:02,680 --> 00:03:05,810
Damn his Vulcan hearing.

47
00:03:07,320 --> 00:03:10,010
You fellows are planning a party for me, aren't you?

48
00:03:10,080 --> 00:03:12,610
Okay, Sheldon, sit down.

49
00:03:12,670 --> 00:03:15,770
If there's going to be a theme I should let you know

50
00:03:15,840 --> 00:03:18,570
that I don't care for luau, toga or "under the sea."

51
00:03:21,030 --> 00:03:22,940
Yeah, we'll keep that in mind. Look...

52
00:03:23,010 --> 00:03:27,110
We need to talk to you about something that happened at the North Pole.

53
00:03:27,180 --> 00:03:29,570
If this is about the night the heat went out,

54
00:03:29,630 --> 00:03:32,900
there's nothing to be embarrassed about.

55
00:03:32,970 --> 00:03:34,520
It's not about that.

56
00:03:34,580 --> 00:03:36,970
And we agreed never speak of it again.

57
00:03:37,040 --> 00:03:38,750
So we slept together naked.

58
00:03:41,680 --> 00:03:44,610
It was only to keep our core body temperatures from plummeting.

59
00:03:46,190 --> 00:03:48,220
He's speaking about it.

60
00:03:48,290 --> 00:03:49,730
For me, it was a bonding moment.

61
00:03:54,750 --> 00:03:57,380
Sheldon, you remember the first few weeks

62
00:03:57,450 --> 00:03:59,790
We were looking for magnetic monopoles

63
00:03:59,820 --> 00:04:02,120
And not finding anything and you were acting

64
00:04:02,190 --> 00:04:04,610
like an obnoxious, giant dictator?

65
00:04:04,670 --> 00:04:06,670
thought we were going to be gentle with him.

66
00:04:06,740 --> 00:04:09,970
That's why I added the "tator."

67
00:04:13,660 --> 00:04:16,030
And then when we finally got our first positive data,

68
00:04:16,090 --> 00:04:17,570
you were so happy.

69
00:04:17,640 --> 00:04:18,910
Oh, yes.

70
00:04:18,990 --> 00:04:22,090
In the world of emoticons, I was colon, capital "D."

71
00:04:28,380 --> 00:04:31,340
Well, in actuality, what your equipment detected

72
00:04:31,410 --> 00:04:35,310
wasn't so much evidence of paradigm-shifting monopoles

73
00:04:35,370 --> 00:04:37,260
as it was...

74
00:04:37,330 --> 00:04:38,840
static from the electric can opener

75
00:04:38,900 --> 00:04:40,520
we were turning on and off.

76
00:04:42,910 --> 00:04:45,560
He just went colon, capital "O."

77
00:04:48,490 --> 00:04:49,580
You tampered with my experiment?

78
00:04:49,650 --> 00:04:50,760
We had to.

79
00:04:50,820 --> 00:04:52,940
It was the only way to keep you

80
00:04:53,010 --> 00:04:55,740
from being such a huge Dickensian.

81
00:04:55,810 --> 00:04:59,540
You see that? I add the "ensian."

82
00:04:59,600 --> 00:05:01,310
Did Leonard know about this?

83
00:05:01,390 --> 00:05:03,180
Leonard's my best friend in the world.

84
00:05:03,240 --> 00:05:04,380
Surely Leonard didn't know.

85
00:05:04,450 --> 00:05:05,700
Actually, it was his idea.

86
00:05:05,760 --> 00:05:08,530
Of course it was. The whole plan weeks of Leonard.

87
00:05:10,200 --> 00:05:12,030
- I missed you so much.  - I missed you, too

88
00:05:12,100 --> 00:05:14,290
I couldn't even think of anyone else while you were gone.

89
00:05:14,360 --> 00:05:16,770
Me, neither.

90
00:05:16,840 --> 00:05:18,770
Except for one night when the heat went out.

91
00:05:20,580 --> 00:05:22,250
Long story. It's... Don't ask.

92
00:05:25,380 --> 00:05:28,150
Leonard.

93
00:05:28,210 --> 00:05:32,930
Do not make a sound.

94
00:05:33,530 --> 00:05:37,200
Whispering, "Do not make a sound..."

95
00:05:37,270 --> 00:05:38,500
...is a sound.

96
00:05:40,260 --> 00:05:42,930
Damn his Vulcan hearing.

97
00:05:42,990 --> 00:05:44,680
Not a good time, Sheldon.

98
00:05:46,260 --> 00:05:47,740
Penny.

99
00:05:47,810 --> 00:05:49,580
Penny. Penny.

100
00:05:49,640 --> 00:05:50,670
Oh, this is ridicules.

101
00:05:52,350 --> 00:05:53,480
What?

102
00:05:53,560 --> 00:05:54,570
Hello, Penny

103
00:05:54,600 --> 00:05:56,170
I realize you're currently at the mercy

104
00:05:56,240 --> 00:05:57,490
of your primitive biological urges,

105
00:05:57,560 --> 00:05:59,000
but as you have an entire lifetime

106
00:05:59,070 --> 00:06:02,100
of poor decisions ahead of you, may I interrupt this one?

107
00:06:03,800 --> 00:06:05,520
It's great to see you too. Come on in.

108
00:06:06,520 --> 00:06:09,800
Wolowitz has informed me of your grand deception.

109
00:06:09,800 --> 00:06:11,580
Do you have anything to say for yourself?

110
00:06:11,640 --> 00:06:14,010
Yes, I feel terrible about it.

111
00:06:14,080 --> 00:06:16,440
I will never forgive myself, I don't expect you to, either,

112
00:06:16,500 --> 00:06:19,670
and I would really appreciate it if you would leave me with Penny

113
00:06:19,670 --> 00:06:23,380
for a session of self-criticism and repentance.

114
00:06:25,380 --> 00:06:27,870
Okay, can someone please tell me what's going on here?

115
00:06:27,950 --> 00:06:30,430
What's going on is I was lead to believe

116
00:06:30,530 --> 00:06:32,550
I was making groundbreaking strides in science,

117
00:06:32,620 --> 00:06:33,960
when in fact, I was being fed

118
00:06:34,030 --> 00:06:36,800
false data at the hands of Wolowitz, Koothrappali

119
00:06:36,870 --> 00:06:39,410
and your furry little boy toy.

120
00:06:40,730 --> 00:06:42,110
Is that true?

121
00:06:42,180 --> 00:06:44,760
It was the only way to make him happy.

122
00:06:44,830 --> 00:06:46,250
Well, why'd you have to make him happy?

123
00:06:46,320 --> 00:06:48,780
Because when he wasn't happy, we wanted to kill him.

124
00:06:50,500 --> 00:06:53,460
There was even a plan.

125
00:06:53,530 --> 00:06:55,750
We were going to throw his kindle outside,

126
00:06:55,810 --> 00:06:57,460
and when he went to get it, lock the door

127
00:06:57,530 --> 00:06:59,150
and let him freeze to death.

128
00:06:59,220 --> 00:07:02,850
That seems like a bit of an overreaction.

129
00:07:02,930 --> 00:07:05,410
No, the overreaction was the plan to tie your limbs

130
00:07:05,480 --> 00:07:07,670
to four different sled dog teams and yell, "Mush."

131
00:07:08,980 --> 00:07:12,130
Look, we kept the original data.

132
00:07:12,210 --> 00:07:14,640
You can still publish the actual results.

133
00:07:14,700 --> 00:07:17,520
Yes, but the actual results are unsuccessful

134
00:07:17,600 --> 00:07:20,550
and I've already sent an e-mail to everyone at the university

135
00:07:20,620 --> 00:07:22,270
explaining that I have confirmed string theory

136
00:07:22,340 --> 00:07:24,760
and forever changed man's understanding of the universe.

137
00:07:24,830 --> 00:07:27,350
Aw, see, yeah, you probably shouldn't have done that.

138
00:07:29,470 --> 00:07:31,760
So write another e-mail. Set the record straight.

139
00:07:31,820 --> 00:07:32,970
It's no big deal.

140
00:07:34,700 --> 00:07:36,120
You're right, Leonard.

141
00:07:36,120 --> 00:07:37,590
It's not a big deal.

142
00:07:37,660 --> 00:07:40,570
All you did was lie to me, destroy my dream

143
00:07:40,630 --> 00:07:43,360
and humiliate me in front of the whole university.

144
00:07:45,590 --> 00:07:48,430
That, FYI, was sarcasm.

145
00:07:49,630 --> 00:07:52,460
I, in fact, believe it is a big deal.

146
00:07:54,460 --> 00:07:55,940
Oh, the poor thing.

147
00:07:56,040 --> 00:07:57,460
Yeah, I feel terrible.

148
00:07:57,460 --> 00:08:00,300
Wait wait, aren't you going to go talk to him?

149
00:08:00,300 --> 00:08:01,670
What? Uh, he'll be fine.

150
00:08:01,740 --> 00:08:03,470
The guy's a trooper. Come here.

151
00:08:03,540 --> 00:08:05,560
No, you're right-- you shouldn't talk to him.

152
00:08:05,620 --> 00:08:07,180
I will.

153
00:08:09,440 --> 00:08:11,690
Man, I cannot catch a break.

154
00:08:20,230 --> 00:08:21,500
Do you want to talk?

155
00:08:21,580 --> 00:08:23,220
About what?

156
00:08:23,290 --> 00:08:25,390
Being betrayed by my friends?

157
00:08:25,460 --> 00:08:28,780
Spending three months at the North Pole for nothing?

158
00:08:28,850 --> 00:08:31,790
And I didn't even get to go to Comic-Con!

159
00:08:35,640 --> 00:08:37,230
Oh, hon...

160
00:08:37,290 --> 00:08:38,780
Uh...

161
00:08:42,780 --> 00:08:46,280
Soft kitty, warm kitty...

162
00:08:46,600 --> 00:08:49,190
That's for when I'm sick. Sad is not sick.

163
00:08:49,260 --> 00:08:50,540
Oh. Sorry.

164
00:08:50,610 --> 00:08:51,860
I don't know your sad song.

165
00:08:51,930 --> 00:08:55,300
I don't have a sad song. I'm not a child.

166
00:08:55,370 --> 00:08:59,480
Well, you know, I do understand what you're going through.

167
00:08:59,540 --> 00:09:00,750
Really?

168
00:09:00,820 --> 00:09:02,880
Did you just have the Nobel Prize in waitressing

169
00:09:02,950 --> 00:09:04,600
stolen from you?

170
00:09:04,670 --> 00:09:08,340
Well, no, but when I was a senior in high school,

171
00:09:08,410 --> 00:09:09,730
one of my friends heard

172
00:09:09,810 --> 00:09:11,720
I was going to be named head cheerleader.

173
00:09:11,780 --> 00:09:13,370
Oh, I was so excited.

174
00:09:13,450 --> 00:09:16,070
My mom even made me a celebration pie.

175
00:09:16,130 --> 00:09:19,060
Then they named stupid Valerie Mosbacher head cheerleader.

176
00:09:19,130 --> 00:09:21,600
Big old slutbag.

177
00:09:21,640 --> 00:09:23,790
Are you saying

178
00:09:23,860 --> 00:09:26,160
that you think a "celebtion pie"

179
00:09:26,230 --> 00:09:29,390
is even remotely comparable to a Nobel Prize?

180
00:09:29,460 --> 00:09:33,030
Well, they're pretty tasty.

181
00:09:33,700 --> 00:09:37,320
And on a different, but not unrelated topic,

182
00:09:37,380 --> 00:09:40,200
based on your current efforts to buoy my spirits,

183
00:09:40,200 --> 00:09:42,570
do you truly believe that you were ever fit

184
00:09:42,640 --> 00:09:45,770
to be a cheer leader?

185
00:09:50,470 --> 00:09:51,810
Look, Sheldon, I just don't think

186
00:09:51,880 --> 00:09:54,140
that the guys and Leonard really meant to hurt you.

187
00:09:54,210 --> 00:09:56,460
You know? They just told an unfortunate lie

188
00:09:56,540 --> 00:09:58,520
to deal with a difficult situation.

189
00:09:58,590 --> 00:10:00,110
Okay, you know what it's like?

190
00:10:00,180 --> 00:10:01,560
Remember that scene in the new Star Trek movie

191
00:10:01,630 --> 00:10:03,530
when Kirk has to take over the ship,

192
00:10:03,590 --> 00:10:05,700
so he tells Spock all that stuff he knew wasn't true,

193
00:10:05,770 --> 00:10:09,290
like saying Spock didn't care his mom died?

194
00:10:09,350 --> 00:10:11,950
I missed Comic- Con and the new Star Trek movie!

195
00:10:16,160 --> 00:10:17,910
I like the new look.

196
00:10:17,980 --> 00:10:19,960
Thanks.

197
00:10:22,360 --> 00:10:24,760
I call it "the Clooney."

198
00:10:24,830 --> 00:10:29,200
I call it "the Mario and Luigi but whatever.

199
00:10:30,630 --> 00:10:32,440
Hey, how's sheldon doing?

200
00:10:32,500 --> 00:10:34,280
Well, he came out of his room this morning

201
00:10:34,280 --> 00:10:35,730
wearing his Darth Vader helmet and

202
00:10:35,730 --> 00:10:37,770
trying to chop me to death with the "force"

203
00:10:37,770 --> 00:10:39,870
so I would say "a little better."

204
00:10:40,470 --> 00:10:42,310
If I may abruptly change the subject,

205
00:10:42,310 --> 00:10:44,480
did you and Penny finally... you know.

206
00:10:44,550 --> 00:10:46,300
- Howard... - Personally, I don't care,

207
00:10:46,300 --> 00:10:48,550
but my genitals wanted me to ask.

208
00:10:48,620 --> 00:10:50,940
Well tell your genitals

209
00:10:50,940 --> 00:10:52,970
what I do with Penny is none of their business.

210
00:10:53,420 --> 00:10:55,910
He says they didn't do it.

211
00:10:57,260 --> 00:10:59,250
Sheldon, over here.

212
00:11:13,630 --> 00:11:14,700
What are you doing?

213
00:11:16,440 --> 00:11:17,750
well, I feel bad for the guy.

214
00:11:20,870 --> 00:11:23,000
Sheldon, why are you sitting by yourself?

215
00:11:23,060 --> 00:11:24,680
Because I am without friends.

216
00:11:24,740 --> 00:11:28,280
Like the proverbial cheese, I stand alone.

217
00:11:28,350 --> 00:11:30,210
Evenhile seated.

218
00:11:30,280 --> 00:11:32,970
Come on. We said we we sorry.

219
00:11:33,040 --> 00:11:34,900
It's going to take more than an "I'm sorry"

220
00:11:34,970 --> 00:11:37,030
and a store-bought apology pie from Penny

221
00:11:37,030 --> 00:11:38,980
to make up for what you've done to me.

222
00:11:39,030 --> 00:11:40,020
Hey, Cooper.

223
00:11:40,090 --> 00:11:41,710
Read your retraction email.

224
00:11:41,780 --> 00:11:44,130
Way to destroy your reputation.

225
00:11:44,200 --> 00:11:45,990
You see?

226
00:11:46,060 --> 00:11:48,610
People have been pointing and laughing at me all morning.

227
00:11:48,680 --> 00:11:50,060
That's not true.

228
00:11:50,130 --> 00:11:52,960
People have been pointing and laughing at you your whole life.

229
00:11:53,030 --> 00:11:54,860
All right, I've had enough.

230
00:11:54,930 --> 00:11:56,270
Attention, everyone.

231
00:11:56,330 --> 00:11:58,100
I'm Dr. Sheldon Cooper.

232
00:11:58,160 --> 00:12:00,460
As many of you in the physics department might know,

233
00:12:00,530 --> 00:12:03,420
my career trajectory has taken a minor detour.

234
00:12:03,490 --> 00:12:05,140
Off a cliff.

235
00:12:06,760 --> 00:12:08,780
My credibility may have been damaged

236
00:12:08,860 --> 00:12:10,040
Completely wrecked.

237
00:12:10,100 --> 00:12:12,900
But I would like to remind you that in science,

238
00:12:12,970 --> 00:12:14,580
there's no such thing as failure.

239
00:12:14,640 --> 00:12:17,990
There once was a man who was referred to his prediction

240
00:12:18,060 --> 00:12:21,170
of a cosmological constant as the single "biggest blunder"

241
00:12:21,240 --> 00:12:23,280
of his career. That man's name was--

242
00:12:23,340 --> 00:12:25,030
surprise, surprise--

243
00:12:25,030 --> 00:12:26,320
Albert Einstein.

244
00:12:27,700 --> 00:12:30,270
Yeah, but research into Dark Energy

245
00:12:30,270 --> 00:12:32,250
proved that Einstein's cosmological constant

246
00:12:32,250 --> 00:12:34,450
was actually right all along, so you're still--

247
00:12:34,520 --> 00:12:37,810
surprise, surprise--

248
00:12:37,810 --> 00:12:39,130
a loser.

249
00:12:40,380 --> 00:12:42,350
Oh, you think you're so clever.

250
00:12:42,420 --> 00:12:43,690
Well, let me just tell you,

251
00:12:43,760 --> 00:12:45,610
while I do not currently have a scathing retort,

252
00:12:45,680 --> 00:12:48,940
you check your e-mail periodically for a doozy.

253
00:12:53,590 --> 00:12:56,180
So much for our friendship with Sheldon.

254
00:12:57,200 --> 00:13:01,260
Well, we always have the night the heat went out.

255
00:13:08,700 --> 00:13:10,430
- Hi.  - Hey.

256
00:13:10,500 --> 00:13:12,030
Listen, since we got, you know, interrupted last night,

257
00:13:12,100 --> 00:13:14,070
I didn't have a chance to give you this.

258
00:13:14,070 --> 00:13:18,640
Oh, Leonard, you shouldn't have.

259
00:13:18,710 --> 00:13:20,310
Oh, boy!

260
00:13:21,670 --> 00:13:23,510
What is it?

261
00:13:23,570 --> 00:13:25,440
It's a snowflake.

262
00:13:25,510 --> 00:13:26,870
From the North Pole.

263
00:13:26,870 --> 00:13:28,970
Are you serious?

264
00:13:29,040 --> 00:13:30,140
Uh-huh.

265
00:13:30,210 --> 00:13:31,270
It'll last forever.

266
00:13:31,340 --> 00:13:34,570
I preserved  in a one percent solution of polyvinyl acetal resin.

267
